# Target C-II — the TB1 inactive-slack branch contains a large near-unit or near-diameter matching

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the `NEAR-ACTIVE` side of the strong-TB1 exact contact-swap fork through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** TB1 exact contact-swap obstruction fork; full-source circumradius bound; unit separation  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_inactive_slack_matching_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The exact contact-swap theorem leaves one new escape for a linear same-sign strong-TB1 family: linearly many distinct previously strict source pairs lie within `O(D^-1)` of either the unit lower constraint or the diameter upper constraint.

That statement can be sharpened without any new KKT input.

Let

\[
s_0={791\over320{,}000{,}000}.
\]

On the `NEAR-ACTIVE` side of the explicit TB1 fork there are more than

\[
{s_0\over4}D-o(D)
\]

distinct witnesses. Therefore one of the two slack types has more than

\[
\boxed{
{s_0\over8}D-o(D)
=(3.08984375\times10^{-7})D-o(D)
}
\]

distinct pairs.

The two cases have different geometry.

1. **Near-unit case.** For sufficiently large `D`, the graph of pairs of length at most
   \[
   1+{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1})
   \]
   has maximum degree at most `6`. Hence the witness family contains a vertex-disjoint matching of size
   \[
   \boxed{
   {s_0\over88}D-o(D)
   =(2.8089488636\ldots\times10^{-8})D-o(D).
   }
   \]

2. **Near-diameter case.** Every pair of length
   \[
   D-O(D^{-1})
   \]
   has midpoint in the same
   \[
   (3^{1/8}+o(1))D^{3/4}
   \]
   ball around the canonical source circumcenter. The near-diameter graph has maximum degree at most
   \[
   (8\,3^{1/8}+o(1))D^{3/4}.
   \]
   Hence the witness family contains a vertex-disjoint matching of size
   \[
   \boxed{
   \left({s_0\over128\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)\right)D^{1/4}
   }
   \]
   with coefficient
   \[
   1.6833575791\ldots\times10^{-8}.
   \]

So the inactive-slack alternative cannot be supported by repeated use of a few vertices. If the lower constraint is responsible, a linear vertex-disjoint almost-contact matching appears. If the upper constraint is responsible, an unbounded vertex-disjoint family of almost-diameter chords appears, all with centrally localized midpoints.

---

## 1. Split the contact-swap witnesses by active boundary

The contact-swap fork gives a set `W_D` of distinct previously strict source pairs with

\[
|W_D|>{s_0\over4}D-o(D),
\tag{IM.1}

such that every pair satisfies either

\[
1<|xy|\le1+q_D
\tag{IM.2}

or

\[
D-q_D\le|xy|<D,
\tag{IM.3}

where

\[
q_D={31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1})
=O(D^{-1}).
\tag{IM.4}

Let `W_D^1` and `W_D^D` denote the two types. At least one has cardinality

\[
\boxed{
>{s_0\over8}D-o(D).
}
\tag{IM.5}

No pair is counted twice for all sufficiently large `D`, since `D\to\infty` while the two distance windows are disjoint.

---

## 2. Near-unit witness graph has degree at most six

Form the graph `G_1(D)` on the source points by joining every pair whose distance lies in

\[
[1,1+q_D].
\]

Fix a vertex `x`. Suppose it had seven distinct neighbors `y_1,...,y_7`. Order their rays around `x`. Two consecutive rays have angular separation at most

\[
{2\pi\over7}.
\tag{IM.6}

Every neighbor radius lies in `[1,1+q_D]`. Put

\[
R_D^{(1)}:=1+q_D.
\]

For the two neighbors with angular gap `phi<=2pi/7`, radially extend each point to radius `R_D^(1)` on the same ray. The triangle inequality gives

\[
|y_j-y_{j+1}|
\le2q_D+2(1+q_D)\sin{\pi\over7}.
\tag{IM.7}

Since `q_D->0` and

\[
2\sin{\pi\over7}=0.867767\ldots<1,
\]

the right side is strictly below `1` for all sufficiently large `D`. This contradicts the source separation condition.

Therefore

\[
\boxed{\Delta(G_1(D))\le6}
\tag{IM.8}

for all sufficiently large sources.

A greedy matching in a graph of maximum degree `6` removes at most

\[
2\cdot6-1=11
\]

edges per chosen edge. Hence any `E`-edge subgraph contains a matching of size at least `E/11`.

If the near-unit case in (IM.5) occurs, `W_D^1` therefore contains a matching of size

\[
\boxed{
>{s_0\over88}D-o(D).
}
\tag{IM.9}

Numerically,

\[
{s_0\over88}
={791\over28{,}160{,}000{,}000}
=2.808948863636\ldots\times10^{-8}.
\tag{IM.10}

The matching edges are previously strict, so their lengths are actually greater than `1`; the closed lower endpoint was used only for the degree estimate.

---

## 3. Near-diameter witnesses have centrally localized midpoints

Now assume the near-diameter case in (IM.5). Let `xy` be one such witness and let

\[
m={x+y\over2},
\qquad
\ell=|x-y|\ge D-q_D.
\]

The full source hull lies in the canonical circumdisk

\[
B(o,R_D),
\qquad
R_D<{D\over2}+\eta_D,
\qquad
\eta_D=3^{1/4}\sqrt D.
\tag{IM.11}

The midpoint identity gives

\[
|m-o|^2
={|x-o|^2+|y-o|^2\over2}-{\ell^2\over4}.
\tag{IM.12}

Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
|m-o|^2
&<\left({D\over2}+\eta_D\right)^2
-{(D-q_D)^2\over4}\\
&=D\eta_D+\eta_D^2+{Dq_D\over2}-{q_D^2\over4}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{IM.13}

Define

\[
L_D'
:=\sqrt{D\eta_D+\eta_D^2+{Dq_D\over2}}.
\tag{IM.14}

Since `q_D=O(D^-1)`, the new term `Dq_D/2=O(1)` is negligible relative to `D eta_D`. Thus

\[
\boxed{
|m-o|<L_D'
=(3^{1/8}+o(1))D^{3/4}.
}
\tag{IM.15}

So inactive pairs only `O(D^-1)` below the diameter inherit the same leading midpoint-localization scale as actual diameter edges.

---

## 4. The near-diameter graph has degree `O(D^(3/4))`

Let `G_D^{near}` join all source pairs with length in

\[
[D-q_D,D].
\]

Fix a vertex `x` and two distinct near-diameter neighbors `y,z`. Put

\[
r=|y-x|,
\qquad
s=|z-x|,
\]

so

\[
D-q_D\le r,s\le D.
\tag{IM.16}

By (IM.15), both midpoints

\[
m_y={x+y\over2},
\qquad
m_z={x+z\over2}
\]

lie in `B(o,L_D')`. Hence

\[
|y-z|=2|m_y-m_z|\le4L_D'.
\tag{IM.17}

Let `theta` be the angle at `x` between the rays to `y` and `z`. For the unit directions

\[
u={y-x\over r},
\qquad
v={z-x\over s},
\]

we have

\[
|u-v|
\le {2|y-z|\over D-q_D}
\le {8L_D'\over D-q_D}.
\tag{IM.18}

Thus, for large `D`,

\[
\theta
\le
\Theta_D'
:=2\arcsin\left({4L_D'\over D-q_D}\right)
=(8\,3^{1/8}+o(1))D^{-1/4}.
\tag{IM.19}

So all near-diameter neighbor directions of one vertex lie in one projective angular interval of width at most `Theta_D'`.

They also have a minimum angular separation. Source separation gives

\[
1\le|y-z|^2
=(r-s)^2+4rs\sin^2{\theta\over2}.
\tag{IM.20}

Because `|r-s|<=q_D` and `rs<=D^2`,

\[
\sin{\theta\over2}
\ge {\sqrt{1-q_D^2}\over2D}
\tag{IM.21}

for all sufficiently large `D` when `q_D<1`. Hence distinct near-diameter neighbor directions are separated by at least

\[
\delta_D'
:=2\arcsin\left({\sqrt{1-q_D^2}\over2D}\right)
={1+o(1)\over D}.
\tag{IM.22}

Packing `delta_D'`-separated directions into an interval of width `Theta_D'` gives

\[
\boxed{
\Delta(G_D^{near})
\le1+{\Theta_D'\over\delta_D'}
\le(8\,3^{1/8}+o(1))D^{3/4}.
}
\tag{IM.23}

---

## 5. The near-diameter witnesses contain an `Omega(D^(1/4))` matching

Apply greedy matching to the witness subgraph with edge set `W_D^D`. A chosen edge meets at most

\[
2\Delta(G_D^{near})-1
\]

witness edges. Combining (IM.5) and (IM.23) yields a matching of size

\[
\begin{aligned}
M_D
&\ge
{|W_D^D|\over2\Delta(G_D^{near})-1}\\
&>
\left(
{s_0\over128\,3^{1/8}}-o(1)
\right)D^{1/4}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{IM.24}

Numerically,

\[
\boxed{
{s_0\over128\,3^{1/8}}
=1.6833575791\ldots\times10^{-8}.
}
\tag{IM.25}

Every edge in this matching is a previously strict nondiameter pair of length `D-O(D^-1)`, and every midpoint satisfies the common central bound (IM.15).

---

## 6. Frontier effect

Combining the contact-swap theorem with this note, a winning strong-TB1 branch now has three concrete geometric realizations:

```text
(A) linear fixed-angle crossing switching-diameter pairs;

(B1) a linear vertex-disjoint matching of inactive almost-unit pairs;

(B2) an Omega(D^(1/4)) vertex-disjoint matching of inactive almost-diameter pairs,
     all with midpoints in one O(D^(3/4)) central ball.
```

The residual inactive-slack branch therefore has genuine matching structure rather than an arbitrary multiset of nearly active constraints.

This still does not close C-II. The near-unit matching is compatible with locally dense packing, and the near-diameter matching is not an active diameter matching. The next useful step is to couple the witness edge's unique layer-incidence tag from the contact swap with the common train order. In the near-diameter case, the midpoint localization makes that comparison particularly close to the active-diameter weave; in the near-unit case, bounded degree makes simultaneous local exchanges more plausible.

**Disposition:** upgraded the linear inactive-slack alternative from a count of distinct near-active pairs to a linear near-unit matching or an `Omega(D^(1/4))` centrally localized near-diameter matching. C-II and Level I remain open.
